WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.953
- How many federal crimes do
you think America actually has?

00:00:02.953 --> 00:00:06.480
1,500, 5,000, 100,000?

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:09.040
What if I told you no one actually knows?

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:09.873
Hooray!

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:11.940
Yeah.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:13.580
But the Department of
Justice actually tried

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:15.040
to count the number of federal laws,

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:17.830
and had to stop because
it was just too hard.

00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:20.150
This means that there's no
textbook that summarizes

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:21.810
the basis for each crime,

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:23.330
what the government has to prove in court

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:25.700
or the potential penalty
for each of these crimes.

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:27.480
In fact, the United States leads the world

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:28.990
in number of people incarcerated.

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:30.500
China is four times bigger,

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:32.940
but it has only 1.6
million people in prison,

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:36.240
while the United States has
2.3 million people behind bars.

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:38.870
We're number one! (sighs)

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:39.910
At the founding of this country,

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:42.670
the Constitution simply
enumerated three federal crimes,

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:45.990
piracy, counterfeiting
and, of course, treason.

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:47.240
But as the decades went by,

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:49.400
Congress delegated its
authority to make laws

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:50.500
to federal agencies,

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:52.830
who went to work writing regulations.

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:55.220
Many of these regulations
were passed for good reasons,

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:57.640
but those reasons either no longer exist

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:00.200
or they've simply been lost to time.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.840
So, let's take some time out
to look at three categories

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:06.195
of the weirdest federal
laws that still exist.

00:01:06.195 --> 00:01:09.130
(curious music)

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:11.440
Sponsored by CuriosityStream and Nebula.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:14.190
So, let's begin our exploration
of some of the craziest laws

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:17.430
that still exist in this
country with the Post Office.

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:20.270
The Post Office is just about
as old as the country itself,

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:22.110
and for most of America's existence,

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:24.220
few crimes were taken more seriously

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:25.610
than screwing with the mail.

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:27.100
The Post Office's unofficial motto

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:28.477
swells with self-importance,

00:01:28.477 --> 00:01:31.507
"Neither snow nor rain nor
heat nor gloom of night

00:01:31.507 --> 00:01:33.687
"stays these couriers
from the swift completion

00:01:33.687 --> 00:01:35.070
"of their appointed rounds."

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:37.950
And in the 1800s, you
could actually be hanged

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:39.090
for stealing the mail.

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:40.750
And that's all well and
good to punish people

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:42.160
who misuse the mail.

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:44.210
But because we live in a nanny state,

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:47.390
where Uncle Sam is always
trying to tell you what to do,

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:48.680
they've trampled on our freedoms

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:50.950
to be able to send people via the mail.

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:52.130
Unbelievable.

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:54.250
You might think that no
one would ever do this,

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:56.660
but there wasn't actually law
prohibited people shipping

00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:59.480
for the first 140 years
of the Post Office.

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:00.800
And back in the day,

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:03.790
lots of people used the
mail service creatively.

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:06.790
For example, Henry Brown was
an enslaved man from Virginia

00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:08.530
who worked with
abolitionists in Pennsylvania

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:11.700
to mail himself out of slavery in 1849.

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:13.830
Brown burned his hand to get out of work,

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:15.630
then packed himself in a shipping crate.

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:18.670
The journey took 27 hours and cost $87,

00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:20.840
about $2,500 in today's money.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:22.860
He stayed free, and became
a well-known speaker,

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:24.300
magician and performer.

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:25.640
And it worked because, back then,

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:27.000
it was legal to ship people.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.160
In fact, it was legal to
ship babies and children

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:31.200
between 1913 and 1920.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.630
The introduction of the
parcel post in 1913,

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:36.790
gave parents exciting new
options in child transportation.

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:38.930
Parcel post allowed people
to ship heavy objects

00:02:38.930 --> 00:02:39.920
for a reduced rate,

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:43.140
and since parcel post was a lot
cheaper than a train ticket,

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:46.480
parents often opted to stick
an address on their kids

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:48.010
and send them with the postman.

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:50.020
For example, a family in Batavia, Ohio,

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:51.650
sent their son to grandma's house,

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:55.080
less than one mile
away, for just 15 cents.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:57.860
But don't worry, they
insured him for 50 bucks.

00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:00.153
I mean, that's just
good financial planning.

00:03:00.153 --> 00:03:02.170
(bright music)

00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:04.300
And in 1914, an Idaho family shipped

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:06.370
their five-year-old daughter
to their grandparents' house,

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:07.800
73 miles away.

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:09.720
She was sent through a
train's mail compartment

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:13.050
with 53 cents of stamps
attached to her jacket.

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:16.390
Now, sure, this seems like child abuse,

00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:18.200
but the reason postal workers were willing

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:19.390
to transport children by mail

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:21.020
was actually fairly touching.

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:22.890
Families knew their postal carriers well

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:25.150
and trusted them to safely
deliver the children.

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:28.000
So, I guess Kevin Costner
was right all along.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:30.490
And Congress let this
slide until June of 1920,

00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:32.420
when it passed a law that
made it a federal crime

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:35.200
to mail humans, regardless of the weight.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:37.110
Now, that doesn't mean that
people have stopped trying

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:38.770
to ship themselves through the mail.

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:41.250
In 2003, a man named Charles McKinley

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:43.730
mailed himself from New
York to Texas in a box.

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:46.130
He was convicted of a
misdemeanor stowaway charge,

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:48.460
in violation of 18 U.S.C. 2199.

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:50.850
But the heavy hand of
tyranny strikes once again,

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:52.900
because living human beings
is not the only thing

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:55.280
that you can't send via the Post Office.

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:58.640
No, you actually can't
send miniature spoons.

00:03:58.640 --> 00:03:59.473
Why?

00:03:59.473 --> 00:04:01.110
Well, the USPS continues
to add to the list

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:02.210
of things you cannot mail,

00:04:02.210 --> 00:04:04.350
which, not surprisingly,
prohibits sending of anything

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:07.080
by mail that is determined
to be drug paraphernalia,

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:09.230
but that includes things
such as miniature spoons

00:04:09.230 --> 00:04:10.770
with level capacities of less than

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:12.530
1/10 of a cubic centimeter.

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:14.720
Turns out that tiny spoons are great

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:16.160
for snorting cocaine.

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:18.840
Yeah, so in 1979, Congress heard testimony

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:20.310
from anti-drug lobbyists

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:21.960
who talked about the
need to get McDonald's

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:23.550
to stop using their very popular,

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:25.930
little coffee-stirring spoons.

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:28.350
These little spoons were
beloved by coffee drinkers

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:30.838
and drug dealers alike.

00:04:30.838 --> 00:04:31.970
♪ I'm lovin' it ♪

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:34.110
- Dealers used to buy them in bundles.

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:35.950
And even today, these
spoons are sought after.

00:04:35.950 --> 00:04:37.300
You can get them on eBay.

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:39.120
But if they're sent through the mail,

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:41.550
you have probably just
committed a federal crime.

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:43.350
And that takes us to the most vile

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:45.890
of all Post Office-related crimes,

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:48.800
and that is that you can't
dress up like a postal carrier.

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:50.640
Yes, there are hundreds of crimes related

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:52.150
to the U.S. Postal Service,

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:53.420
but the worst is, of course,

00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:55.290
wearing a postal carrier uniform,

00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:57.600
which can get you up
to six months in prison

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:00.030
under 18 U.S.C. 1730.

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:03.377
18 U.S.C. 1730 says,
"Whoever, not being connected

00:05:03.377 --> 00:05:06.257
"with the letter-carrier
branch of the Postal Service,

00:05:06.257 --> 00:05:08.797
"wears the uniform or badge
which may be prescribed

00:05:08.797 --> 00:05:11.507
"by the postal service to be
worn by the letter carriers,

00:05:11.507 --> 00:05:13.737
"shall be fined under
this title or imprisoned

00:05:13.737 --> 00:05:16.050
"not more than six months, or both."

00:05:16.050 --> 00:05:18.090
Eventually, Congress passed
a law allowing actors

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:19.660
to wear USPS uniforms,

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:21.210
but only under the condition

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:23.560
that the production
portray the postal carrier

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:24.950
in a positive light,

00:05:24.950 --> 00:05:26.870
so obviously, like this.

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:29.520
Now, if this strikes you as super-hot

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:32.820
but also unconstitutional,
you're absolutely right.

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:34.420
The U.S. once made it illegal for actors

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:35.610
to wear military uniforms,

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:37.350
unless the production showed the military

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:38.620
in a positive manner.

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:40.080
However, the law was struck down

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:41.530
by the Supreme Court in 1970,

00:05:41.530 --> 00:05:43.680
since allowing actors to wear uniforms

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:46.310
only if the rule did not
discredit the military

00:05:46.310 --> 00:05:48.250
was a violation of the
actor's 1st Amendment right

00:05:48.250 --> 00:05:49.200
to free speech.

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:50.690
And even though that law was struck down,

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:53.690
it took Congress 21 more years
to remove the same language

00:05:53.690 --> 00:05:55.090
from the postal worker statute.

00:05:55.090 --> 00:05:57.107
The law now reads, "The
provisions of the proceeding

00:05:57.107 --> 00:05:59.247
"paragraph shall not apply
to an actor or actress

00:05:59.247 --> 00:06:01.787
"in a theatrical, television
or motion picture production

00:06:01.787 --> 00:06:03.177
"who wears the uniform or badge

00:06:03.177 --> 00:06:04.897
"of the letter-carrier
branch of the Postal Service

00:06:04.897 --> 00:06:07.330
"while portraying the
member of that service."

00:06:07.330 --> 00:06:09.770
Which, of course, is how we got this guy.

00:06:09.770 --> 00:06:12.640
- I'll tell you a little
secret about zip codes.

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:13.960
They're meaningless.

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:15.160
- Newman.

00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:16.440
But let's talk about the truth

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:18.450
and consequences in food regulation,

00:06:18.450 --> 00:06:20.390
because often, lawyers are literally paid

00:06:20.390 --> 00:06:22.000
to engage in food fights.

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:24.570
People can be convicted of
many kinds of food crime,

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:25.980
because the U.S. regulates food,

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:28.130
cosmetics and drugs pretty heavily.

00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:30.340
But understanding which
agencies regulate food

00:06:30.340 --> 00:06:33.140
is very, very complicated,
even for lawyers,

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:34.950
so let's talk about the
jurisdictional battles

00:06:34.950 --> 00:06:36.070
of the food cops.

00:06:36.070 --> 00:06:37.990
There are two federal
agencies that regulate food.

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:39.960
There's the Food and Drug Administration

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:42.100
and the U.S. Department of Agriculture.

00:06:42.100 --> 00:06:44.310
The FDA is responsible
for regulating labels

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:47.030
on all processed foods
created and sold in the U.S.

00:06:47.030 --> 00:06:50.010
The USDA oversees mostly
meat, poultry and eggs.

00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:52.530
The FDA regulates about 75%
of America's food supply

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:54.050
and the USDA has the rest.

00:06:54.050 --> 00:06:55.700
And the division between the two agencies

00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:58.400
makes for some delicious
jurisdictional battles.

00:06:58.400 --> 00:06:59.240
Take eggs.

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:00.350
Whole eggs, shells and all,

00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:01.760
are the responsibility of the FDA,

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:04.200
but liquid eggs are regulated by the USDA.

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:06.590
This might seem strange
since an egg is composed

00:07:06.590 --> 00:07:07.880
of a shell and some liquid.

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:09.070
Why divide them?

00:07:09.070 --> 00:07:10.630
But this complicated division exists

00:07:10.630 --> 00:07:12.090
throughout the food regulation regime.

00:07:12.090 --> 00:07:13.920
For example, the FDA has jurisdiction

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:15.500
over non-domesticated poultry,

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:17.400
like wild turkeys, ducks and geese.

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:19.620
Whereas the USDA regulates
domesticated poultry,

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:23.030
like turkey, chickens, and
yes, domestic geese and ducks.

00:07:23.030 --> 00:07:24.350
But things got even more complicated

00:07:24.350 --> 00:07:25.920
when we talk about sandwiches.

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:28.380
Bread is a bakery product
that is regulated by the FDA,

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:30.910
but meat, like ham or turkey or beef,

00:07:30.910 --> 00:07:32.430
is regulated by the USDA.

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:34.920
So, who regulates a
simple turkey sandwich?

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:37.200
Well, this is where things
get really interesting.

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:39.210
And by interesting, I mean, complicated.

00:07:39.210 --> 00:07:42.230
The FDA regulates a sandwich
with two slices of bread.

00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:43.757
This is because the FDA regulates bread.

00:07:43.757 --> 00:07:45.390
But take away one slice of bread

00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:46.620
and make it an open-face sandwich,

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:49.620
and the USDA steps in since
the ratio of meat to bread

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:51.170
and other ingredients is more than half.

00:07:51.170 --> 00:07:52.300
And this is no laughing matter,

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:53.920
the food cops are deadly serious about it.

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:55.890
Here's a notice from the
USDA about inspection

00:07:55.890 --> 00:07:58.610
of closed-face sandwiches that
are on their way to Canada.

00:07:58.610 --> 00:08:01.330
And the agency knows that
places like White Castle or KFC

00:08:01.330 --> 00:08:03.750
might shove meat between
two biscuits or pancakes,

00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:06.260
or even chicken between
two slices of chicken,

00:08:06.260 --> 00:08:07.770
so they have that covered as well.

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:10.960
But perhaps the biggest food
related tragedy in all of law

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:14.040
is that you can't make food with love.

00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:15.990
That's right, companies
that get a little too cheeky

00:08:15.990 --> 00:08:18.680
with the way that they list
ingredients on their food

00:08:18.680 --> 00:08:20.560
could be looking at a prison sentence.

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:22.460
Check out this mean letter from the FDA

00:08:22.460 --> 00:08:24.450
to the Nashoba Brooke's Bakery,

00:08:24.450 --> 00:08:26.700
which had the audacity to include love

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:28.127
as an ingredient in its bread.

00:08:28.127 --> 00:08:30.017
"Your Nashoba Granola
and Whole Wheat Bread,

00:08:30.017 --> 00:08:31.677
"wholesale and retail,
products are misbranded

00:08:31.677 --> 00:08:33.797
"within the meaning of
section 403 of the Act.

00:08:33.797 --> 00:08:35.607
"For example, your Nashoba Granola label

00:08:35.607 --> 00:08:36.637
"lists ingredient, 'Love.'

00:08:36.637 --> 00:08:38.957
"Ingredients required to
be declared on the label

00:08:38.957 --> 00:08:40.387
"or labeling of food must be listed

00:08:40.387 --> 00:08:42.127
"by their common or usual name.

00:08:42.127 --> 00:08:45.067
"Love is not a common or
usual name of an ingredient,

00:08:45.067 --> 00:08:47.137
"and is considered to
be intervening material

00:08:47.137 --> 00:08:49.817
"because it is not part of
the common or usual name

00:08:49.817 --> 00:08:50.840
"of the ingredient.

00:08:50.840 --> 00:08:51.920
That's right, Nashoba,

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:53.347
the government has questions.

00:08:53.347 --> 00:08:55.427
"Just what is this love ingredient?

00:08:55.427 --> 00:08:57.867
"How much love is in a pound of granola?

00:08:57.867 --> 00:09:00.467
"Is love measured in
grams, ounces or pounds?

00:09:00.467 --> 00:09:02.697
"What kind of hippy bread and
granola are you cooking up

00:09:02.697 --> 00:09:04.060
"over there in Massachusetts?"

00:09:04.060 --> 00:09:06.330
Thankfully, for Nashoba,
the FDA encourages

00:09:06.330 --> 00:09:09.250
self-correction of alleged
violations of the FDCA

00:09:09.250 --> 00:09:10.410
over criminal prosecution,

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:12.350
and generally, if a
violation is suspected,

00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:14.490
the FDA will provide the alleged offender

00:09:14.490 --> 00:09:17.250
with the opportunity to
correct unlawful conduct

00:09:17.250 --> 00:09:18.430
before taking further action,

00:09:18.430 --> 00:09:22.160
and to remove all love from your products.

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:23.780
However, the government regulators

00:09:23.780 --> 00:09:25.220
could opt for prison time

00:09:25.220 --> 00:09:28.230
if you demand to add love to your food.

00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:31.440
A first offense conviction of
a mislabeled consumer product

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:33.410
might carry a maximum sentence of one year

00:09:33.410 --> 00:09:35.500
in federal prison or a $1,000 fine,

00:09:35.500 --> 00:09:37.730
and the text of the statute
allows the FDA to charge both.

00:09:37.730 --> 00:09:39.040
Hopefully, the government recognizes

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:40.780
that there's a difference
between selling eggs

00:09:40.780 --> 00:09:42.200
with the incorrect date for the purpose

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:43.900
of passing off old eggs as fresh,

00:09:43.900 --> 00:09:45.810
and labeling your granola as containing

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:47.320
lots and lots of love.

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:50.290
Obviously, the love is much, much worse.

00:09:50.290 --> 00:09:51.123
But don't worry,

00:09:51.123 --> 00:09:53.410
as long as they don't sell and label them,

00:09:53.410 --> 00:09:55.370
Grandma can still bake your cookies

00:09:55.370 --> 00:09:58.800
with as much love as
she wants, from prison.

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:00.480
All right, we've talked
about the Post Office,

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:02.180
we've talked about eliminating the scourge

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:05.580
that is the love that is
pervasive in all of our foods,

00:10:05.580 --> 00:10:07.470
and let's talk about the final category

00:10:07.470 --> 00:10:10.900
of crazy federal laws,
that is weather laws.

00:10:10.900 --> 00:10:12.500
And if you've enjoyed this video so far,

00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:13.880
please give it a like so we can make sure

00:10:13.880 --> 00:10:16.100
that more people know
about all the crazy laws

00:10:16.100 --> 00:10:17.850
that still exist in this country.

00:10:17.850 --> 00:10:19.980
But it's also important that you know

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:22.100
not to falsify a weather report.

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:23.580
It's actually a violation of federal law

00:10:23.580 --> 00:10:25.980
to falsify a National
Weather Service forecast

00:10:25.980 --> 00:10:27.670
and pass it off as official.

00:10:27.670 --> 00:10:30.250
Not that any high-ranking
government official

00:10:30.250 --> 00:10:32.830
has ever falsified a weather report.

00:10:32.830 --> 00:10:35.560
In fact, if one were crazy enough

00:10:35.560 --> 00:10:38.890
to take a Sharpie and falsify
a National Weather Report,

00:10:38.890 --> 00:10:40.290
you could actually get 90 days in jail

00:10:40.290 --> 00:10:41.260
for breaking this law.

00:10:41.260 --> 00:10:44.237
18 U.S.C. 2074 reads,
"Whoever knowingly issues

00:10:44.237 --> 00:10:46.347
"or publishes any
counterfeit weather forecast

00:10:46.347 --> 00:10:48.427
"or warning of weather conditions falsely

00:10:48.427 --> 00:10:50.187
"representing such forecast or warning

00:10:50.187 --> 00:10:52.277
"to have been issued or
published by the Weather Bureau,

00:10:52.277 --> 00:10:53.367
"United States Signal Service

00:10:53.367 --> 00:10:54.757
"or their branch of the government service

00:10:54.757 --> 00:10:56.627
"shall be fined under
this title or imprisoned

00:10:56.627 --> 00:10:58.630
"not more than 90 days, or both."

00:10:58.630 --> 00:11:00.220
This might seem like a trivial thing.

00:11:00.220 --> 00:11:02.500
However, if an official violates this law,

00:11:02.500 --> 00:11:03.540
it's a serious matter.

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:05.970
The National Oceanic and
Atmospheric Administration's

00:11:05.970 --> 00:11:08.140
National Weather Service, the NWS,

00:11:08.140 --> 00:11:09.640
is the government agency that provides

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:11.860
weather forecast and
reports across this country.

00:11:11.860 --> 00:11:13.490
And giving out false information

00:11:13.490 --> 00:11:15.620
can interfere with the farming
and shipping industries,

00:11:15.620 --> 00:11:18.830
and could even cause people to
put themselves in harm's way.

00:11:18.830 --> 00:11:21.060
And for a practical
application of this law

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:23.110
that probably doesn't
get a whole lot of use,

00:11:23.110 --> 00:11:25.590
we need look no further
than former President Trump,

00:11:25.590 --> 00:11:28.270
who caused a controversy by
insisting that hurricane Dorian

00:11:28.270 --> 00:11:31.320
was tracking towards
Alabama, when it was not.

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:33.123
Trump made this claim via tweet.

00:11:34.370 --> 00:11:35.580
What, you thought I was going to say

00:11:35.580 --> 00:11:37.490
that he made it in a
well-rehearsed speech?

00:11:37.490 --> 00:11:38.860
No, he made it in a tweet.

00:11:38.860 --> 00:11:40.250
But the National Hurricane Center did not,

00:11:40.250 --> 00:11:42.300
at any point, include
Alabama in it's forecast,

00:11:42.300 --> 00:11:44.190
for where Dorian would make landfall.

00:11:44.190 --> 00:11:46.560
The NWS Birmingham quickly
corrected the President

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:48.830
in assuring people that they
didn't need to evacuate.

00:11:48.830 --> 00:11:51.200
But contradicting the
President was a big no-no

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:52.220
during the Trump era.

00:11:52.220 --> 00:11:54.950
Rule number one was, never
contradict the President.

00:11:54.950 --> 00:11:56.740
And, of course, an angry President Trump

00:11:56.740 --> 00:11:58.827
dug in and defended
himself by saying that,

00:11:58.827 --> 00:12:01.840
"Days earlier, the path of the
hurricane included Alabama,"

00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:04.130
and produced a chart
with a Sharpie drawing.

00:12:04.130 --> 00:12:06.030
The National Weather
Service was under pressure

00:12:06.030 --> 00:12:07.460
not to contradict President Trump,

00:12:07.460 --> 00:12:09.600
so it ended up putting
up false information

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:11.220
to cover the president.

00:12:11.220 --> 00:12:13.030
But the law wasn't actually tailor made

00:12:13.030 --> 00:12:14.430
to former President Trump.

00:12:14.430 --> 00:12:17.000
Back when meteorology
was still a new science,

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:19.093
amateurs were selling weather
forecasts all the time.

00:12:19.093 --> 00:12:23.080
- It's 68 degrees, and
there's a 30% chance

00:12:23.080 --> 00:12:24.520
that it's already raining.

00:12:24.520 --> 00:12:26.470
- This was problematic,
especially when they asserted

00:12:26.470 --> 00:12:28.480
that the information was coming
from the U.S. government.

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:30.720
So, the government's major
concern was that people

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:33.210
would hold the government
accountable for bogus reports.

00:12:33.210 --> 00:12:34.990
Therefore, they passed
laws making it illegal

00:12:34.990 --> 00:12:36.900
to falsify a government weather report.

00:12:36.900 --> 00:12:39.550
But at an even more fundamental level,

00:12:39.550 --> 00:12:40.730
you actually can't change

00:12:40.730 --> 00:12:43.220
the weather itself without permission.

00:12:43.220 --> 00:12:45.520
For example, in 15 U.S.C. 330,

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:47.710
it actually prohibits anyone
from modifying the weather,

00:12:47.710 --> 00:12:50.157
quote, "No person may
engage or attempt to engage

00:12:50.157 --> 00:12:52.367
"in any weather modification
activities in the United States

00:12:52.367 --> 00:12:54.667
"unless he submits to
the Secretary of Commerce

00:12:54.667 --> 00:12:56.457
"such reports with respect to thereto,

00:12:56.457 --> 00:12:58.767
"in such form and
containing such information,

00:12:58.767 --> 00:13:01.240
"as the Secretary by rule prescribe."

00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:03.210
Section 330 gives the
Secretary of Commerce

00:13:03.210 --> 00:13:06.010
the power to subpoena anyone
caught modifying the weather

00:13:06.010 --> 00:13:07.450
without proper notification.

00:13:07.450 --> 00:13:10.610
If a person defies the
Secretary of Commerce subpoena,

00:13:10.610 --> 00:13:13.070
the court may find that person
to contempt and jail them.

00:13:13.070 --> 00:13:14.500
And contrary to popular belief,

00:13:14.500 --> 00:13:17.750
this law was not invented to
fight the My Little Ponies,

00:13:17.750 --> 00:13:19.000
rather the Genesis of this law

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:20.960
is actually a process called cloud-seeding

00:13:20.960 --> 00:13:22.750
that was started in the 1940s.

00:13:22.750 --> 00:13:24.970
It involved sending small
aircraft into the clouds

00:13:24.970 --> 00:13:26.440
to effectively modify them,

00:13:26.440 --> 00:13:29.110
and inserting chemicals
to enhance precipitation,

00:13:29.110 --> 00:13:30.790
dissipate fog, modify hurricanes

00:13:30.790 --> 00:13:33.050
and decrease lightning
and hail in thunderstorms.

00:13:33.050 --> 00:13:35.590
But cloud-seeding is a
pretty controversial act

00:13:35.590 --> 00:13:37.890
and it's officially banned in warfare.

00:13:37.890 --> 00:13:40.350
This is partially because
the U.S. used it in Vietnam

00:13:40.350 --> 00:13:41.840
to extend the monsoon season,

00:13:41.840 --> 00:13:43.560
which stopped the North Vietnamese

00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:45.780
from moving troops and
materials southward.

00:13:45.780 --> 00:13:48.350
So, yes, the weather can be altered,

00:13:48.350 --> 00:13:50.560
but you can only do it
with government permission.

00:13:50.560 --> 00:13:51.470
And that's not surprising,

00:13:51.470 --> 00:13:52.690
'cause you need government permission

00:13:52.690 --> 00:13:54.010
for a whole bunch of stuff,

